    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1022">

                <pb facs="0772" n="656" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg656"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 412.00/37.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Brazilian Minister to the Secretary of State.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Brazilian Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-11-11">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">November 11, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>203. Carranza issued regulations through War Office, which have now been
                    communicated to the Diplomatic Corps, to the effect— <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>That for the better investigation of facts relative to claims presented
                            by foreigners it is desirable to require them to prove their nationality
                            as well as their lawful ownership of property claimed.</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>I am under the impression that such exaction can not be admitted, because such
                    proof can be investigated only by the ministers or consuls. Their statement to
                    that is and should be accepted as conclusive. However, I would like to know
                    Department’s views before answering.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cardoso de Oliveira</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
